WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.739 --> 00:00:18.920
out the window, I saw Moriarty frantically waving for the train to stop.

2
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:24.121
It was too late, however, as we were rapidly gaining speed and soon left the station behind.

3
00:00:24.141 --> 00:00:27.562
“With all our precautions, we still cut it rather close.”

4
00:00:28.543 --> 00:00:31.864
Holmes laughed as he took off his disguise and packed it away in a suitcase.

5
00:00:33.548 --> 00:00:35.168
“Have you read the morning paper, Watson?”

6
00:00:36.189 --> 00:00:38.509
“No, I didn’t have time to read anything before I left home.”

7
00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.990
“Then you haven’t heard about Baker Street.

8
00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.551
One of Moriarty’s men set fire to my rooms last night!

9
00:00:45.371 --> 00:00:48.112
Luckily, the fire was put out quickly before it did any damage.”

10
00:00:48.132 --> 00:00:50.072
“Good heavens, Holmes!

11
00:00:51.073 --> 00:00:52.153
This is intolerable!”

12
00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:57.254
I was quite upset, but Holmes seemed neither troubled nor surprised.

13
00:00:57.274 --> 00:01:03.136
“The gang probably lost track of me after their man was arrested for attacking me with a club,”

14
00:01:03.564 --> 00:01:04.085
Holmes said.

15
00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:07.427
“Otherwise they would have known that I didn’t go home.

16
00:01:08.308 --> 00:01:11.411
But they were obviously watching you and followed you to Victoria Station.

17
00:01:12.352 --> 00:01:13.673
Are you sure you didn’t slip up?”

18
00:01:14.494 --> 00:01:18.557
“I followed your instructions exactly,” I said with some irritation at his accusation.

19
00:01:18.577 --> 00:01:21.399
“I sent my luggage to the station last night.

20
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.942
I didn’t take the first cab that arrived.

21
00:01:24.882 --> 00:01:28.185
I ran through the market and arrived precisely at quarter past nine.

22
00:01:29.046 --> 00:01:31.108
I jumped into the carriage that was waiting for me.”

23
00:01:32.044 --> 00:01:33.444
“Did you recognize your coachman?

24
00:01:34.385 --> 00:01:35.625
That was my brother, Mycroft!

25
00:01:36.445 --> 00:01:40.466
I used him rather than some unknown person who could be bribed to reveal our destination.”

26
00:01:42.527 --> 00:01:43.907
Holmes gave me a triumphant look.

27
00:01:43.927 --> 00:01:45.808
“But enough about this morning.

28
00:01:46.548 --> 00:01:48.768
We must plan what we’re going to do about Moriarty.”

29
00:01:49.449 --> 00:01:52.169
We were on the express train that connected with a boat for France.

30
00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.990
“Surely there’s no way for Moriarty to catch us,” I said.

31
00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:01.452
“My dear Watson, you obviously didn’t take me seriously when I said this villain is as clever as

32
00:02:01.572 --> 00:02:01.812
I am.

33
00:02:02.764 --> 00:02:04.164
What would I do in this situation?”

34
00:02:05.145 --> 00:02:06.065
I thought for a moment.

35
00:02:07.065 --> 00:02:08.986
“Hire a special train to follow us, of course.

36
00:02:09.926 --> 00:02:12.427
But we have a good head start, and the express doesn’t stop.

37
00:02:13.287 --> 00:02:16.368
No matter how fast his train goes, it won’t catch up to ours.”

38
00:02:16.388 --> 00:02:22.450
“Ah, but our train does make one stop at Canterbury, and the boat never leaves on time.

39
00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.091
Moriarty will find us at the dock.”

40
00:02:26.811 --> 00:02:28.252
“We’re being chased like we’re the criminals!”

41
00:02:28.732 --> 00:02:29.172
I grumbled.

42
00:02:29.976 --> 00:02:32.598
For a few minutes, I stared out the window at the passing scenery.

43
00:02:32.618 --> 00:02:35.479
“Let’s have Moriarty arrested when he arrives.”

44
00:02:35.499 --> 00:02:37.260
“Absolutely not.”

45
00:02:38.261 --> 00:02:39.782
Holmes shook his head at my suggestion.

46
00:02:39.802 --> 00:02:42.803
“That would throw away three months of my hard work.

47
00:02:43.844 --> 00:02:48.747
If Moriarty were arrested, his gang would know that something was amiss, and they would scatter to who

48
00:02:48.787 --> 00:02:49.287
knows where.

49
00:02:50.368 --> 00:02:54.110
No, we must wait until Monday when the police will arrest Moriarty and his gang.”

50
00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.571
“Well, then what are we supposed to do?

51
00:02:56.871 --> 00:02:57.572
Let him catch us?”

52
00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.022
By this point I was completely frustrated by Moriarty, and I’d never even met the professor.

53
00:03:03.842 --> 00:03:07.364
Holmes’ plan was to get off the train at Canterbury, leaving our luggage behind.

54
00:03:08.985 --> 00:03:12.906
When Moriarty arrived in Paris, he would note that our suitcases had also arrived.

55
00:03:13.627 --> 00:03:15.628
But he would wait in vain for us to show up.

56
00:03:16.668 --> 00:03:20.830
Meanwhile we would take a train to a different port, from which we would cross the English Channel.

57
00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:25.352
Then we would travel to Switzerland, purchasing new clothing and luggage along the way.

58
00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:30.882
So, we left the train at Canterbury, only to discover that the next train wouldn’t arrive for an

59
00:03:30.942 --> 00:03:36.005
hour. I unhappily watched our first train disappear around the bend with my suitcase onboard.

60
00:03:37.086 --> 00:03:41.068
Suddenly Holmes pulled on my sleeve and pointed toward a plume of smoke on the track.

61
00:03:42.128 --> 00:03:43.689
“Look! He’s coming already!”

62
00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:44.690
Holmes cried.

63
00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.532
A minute later an engine pulling a single car passed us with a rattle and a roar.

64
00:03:51.634 --> 00:03:53.835
We barely had time to hide behind a pile of luggage.

65
00:03:54.755 --> 00:03:55.996
Holmes laughed gleefully.

66
00:03:56.888 --> 00:03:59.610
“There are limits to the professor’s intelligence.

67
00:04:00.531 --> 00:04:03.012
He failed to figure out that I would figure out his plans!”

68
00:04:04.033 --> 00:04:05.674
I stood up and brushed myself off.

69
00:04:05.694 --> 00:04:08.816
“And what would he have done if he had found us?”

70
00:04:08.836 --> 00:04:13.159
“He would have tried to kill me,” Holmes replied, showing no emotion.

71
00:04:13.179 --> 00:04:15.881
“Shall we see if we can find some lunch?”

72
00:04:17.282 --> 00:04:21.945
Once again I admired my friend’s bravery as well as his skill in outwitting such a wily enemy.

73
00:04:22.806 --> 00:04:26.208
That night we traveled to Belgium to further throw Moriarty off our trail.

74
00:04:27.072 --> 00:04:29.874
By Monday morning we were in a French city along the Rhine River.

75
00:04:30.775 --> 00:04:34.097
From there Holmes telegraphed Inspector Lestrade at Scotland Yard.

76
00:04:35.818 --> 00:04:38.400
That evening we found a reply waiting for us at our hotel.

77
00:04:39.281 --> 00:04:41.582
Holmes read it and threw it into the fireplace with a groan.

78
00:04:42.043 --> 00:04:43.043
“What’s wrong?”

79
00:04:43.404 --> 00:04:43.844
I asked.

80
00:04:43.864 --> 00:04:45.825
“Moriarty has escaped!

81
00:04:46.566 --> 00:04:50.609
The police captured most of his gang, but the professor and some of his most dangerous men got

82
00:04:50.649 --> 00:04:55.112
away. Of course after I left the country, there was no one equal to dealing with him.

83
00:04:55.768 --> 00:04:57.849
But I thought Lestrade could handle a simple arrest.”

84
00:04:58.670 --> 00:04:59.410
“What happens now?”

85
00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:00.311
I asked.

86
00:05:00.971 --> 00:05:05.553
“With most of his gang in jail, Moriarty won’t have enough people to carry out his plans,” Holmes

87
00:05:05.593 --> 00:05:09.996
said. “We’ve pretty much put him out of business, and he can’t return to London because he knows

88
00:05:10.036 --> 00:05:10.996
the police are after him.

89
00:05:12.998 --> 00:05:15.639
So he’ll devote all his efforts to finding and killing me.

90
00:05:16.499 --> 00:05:20.802
I think you’d better return to London, Watson, because I’m an even more dangerous companion now.”

91
00:05:21.542 --> 00:05:24.444
There was no way I could allow my friend to continue to travel without me.

92
00:05:25.452 --> 00:05:26.593
He was in too much danger.

93
00:05:27.574 --> 00:05:32.338
We argued about the matter throughout dinner, but later that night we resumed our travels to Switzerland together.

94
00:05:33.319 --> 00:05:37.943
For a week we had a pleasant journey, wandering through Alpine villages and lonely mountain passes.

95
00:05:38.704 --> 00:05:43.008
While spring was arriving in the valleys below, the mountains above were still topped with snow.

96
00:05:43.969 --> 00:05:47.312
No matter where we were, Holmes never forgot the danger that hounded us.

97
00:05:48.072 --> 00:05:52.056
He scrutinized every face that passed us, looking for some sign of Moriarty.

98
00:05:53.872 --> 00:05:57.413
One day as we walked along the edge of a lake, a large rock fell from the ridge

99
00:05:57.433 --> 00:05:59.253
above and rolled into the water.

100
00:06:00.274 --> 00:06:03.995
Holmes instantly shot to the top of the ridge, craning his neck in every direction.

101
00:06:05.155 --> 00:06:06.996
Our guide and I followed more slowly.

102
00:06:07.016 --> 00:06:10.637
“There’s nothing wrong,” the guide said, trying to reassure him.

103
00:06:11.437 --> 00:06:14.377
“It’s quite common for rocks to fall at that spot in the spring.”

104
00:06:15.218 --> 00:06:16.778
Holmes merely grunted in reply.

105
00:06:17.878 --> 00:06:21.959
But he raised his eyebrows at me, and I knew he was remembering the falling brick that had

106
00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:22.920
almost hit him in
